<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shafi'i school</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shafi'i</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shafi'i school is one of the four major schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Islamic law, named after the jurist al-Shafi'i. It became widespread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamiyya">Nizamiyya</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> institutionalised Shafi'i jurisprudence, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> followed Shafi'i and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> law in its legal administration.</p></div>
